\f4\fs26 He awoke at home, in bed, alone. The all too familiar alcohol haze pounded an entirely unpleasant African-style beat in his head that became exponentially louder (and proportionately unpleasant) when he opened his eyes, but this proved as pointless as it was painful. One eye was almost entirely swollen over and the good one refused to focus. Eyes firmly shut he carefully untangled himself from the sheets.\
He gingerly placed his feet on the carpet, but somehow the gentle clash of calloused flesh and cushioned floor was far too loud and another tribe of drummers were awoken in his head. After an earnest trial and error session he presently found a combination of moaning a lot and crawling was his best method of transport on the epic journey to the bathroom. He set up base camp at the foot of the bed and with half a bottle of water and a wet towel to slide on so he didn\'92t get carpet burn on his dick, he set forth to bivouac at the doorway. After only two water stops the brown, brackish liquid in the bottle was gone, but he was at the door and made the brave decision to push on and make the final assault on the bathroom. In any case, he could stay no longer, there was a real chance of his tongue would remain permanently stuck to the roof of his mouth. His choice of a wet towel proved to be a winner as the floor to the bathroom was covered in a cheap-shit-plastic faux-linoleum that managed to be both offensively ugly and dangerously slippery, dry or wet, but especially wet. He slid along, burnt and battered, inch-worming and moaning in a low mournful base tone that would have sounded really cool with an African beat. \
\'93Is that a weird sex thing or are you cleaning the floor?\'94 Sarah laughed with delight, but all too loudly for Cracker. \'93Please tell me it\'92s a weird sex thing.\'94 \
\'93Can you give me a bit of a hand?\'94 Cracker managed between moans. \'93I think I might be dying.\'94\
\'93Men such wimps. C\'92mon let\'92s get you up.\'94 She rolled her eyes but helped him to his feet and they both made the final few steps to the bathroom where she left him to his misery. He vomited a voluminous and vile mound of something he could not readily identify and then endured an attack of the beer splatters, but before long he was showered and almost human. Well, not really too close to human. Most of the hair on the left side of his head had been burnt to black stubble and the scalp was raw and heavily blistered. His blackened left eye was firmly closed shut and continually seeped a dilute blood-ooze that trickled over the collection of lumps, cuts, scratches and bruises that together contrived to construct something not too dissimilar to a face.\
As he exited the bathroom he said, \'93I have a couple of questions. Firstly, what the fuck happened last night? I remember the Elvis bar, but not much after that. Did I really try to eat my own face?\'94\
Cracker found Sarah serenely curled up on the lounge, sipping a hot beverage and reading her personal com-screen. She had no injuries at all, in fact she was positively glowing and did not seem to be in any pain or discomfort, from either the expected hangover or from the unexpected explosion. \
\'93Yes, you bet Elvine you could eat your own face. You said it was already half cooked so it would be easy. One of the funniest things I have ever seen. In fact I have never laughed so much in my life.\'94\
\'93I hate to be a little indelicate, but did we?\'94\
\'93No. I am not that sort of girl.\'94 She said with grin.\
\'93A bit below your station perhaps?\'94\
\'93Well that is a bit of a problem, but no. I am just a little old fashioned, that\'92s all.\'94\
\'93Old fashioned? What does that mean? You\'92re not a virgin are you?\'94 He joked, laughing loudly. \'93I don\'92t think I have ever met a member of your tribe.\'94 He continued to laugh as he truly had never met an adult virgin before, certainly no-one that would lay claim to it.\
\'93Yes I am.\'94 She was serious.\
\'93No-one has ever?\'94\
\'93No.\'94\
\'93You\'92ve never ridden the baloney pony?\'94\
\'93No. I would suggest, however, that we focus on what happened last night rather than what did not happen. The bomb at the bar and the flooding of half the city, perhaps?\'94\
\'93Are you waiting for a brave prince on a white steed?\'94 He hoped she would laugh.\
\'93NO!!\'94 This time, very loudly. She then smiled, her face a picture of serenity.\
\'93Okay. I give up.\'94 He looked at the small computer device in her hand. \'93Been surfing the link, eh? What\'92s in the news?\'94\
\'93We are, of course.\'94 She seemed pleased. \'93Last night\'92s events have not gone unnoticed.\'94\
 \'93What does it say?\'94\
\'93They say the bomb at the bar was part of a coordinated attack, involving a cyber-vandalism on the water infra-structure that resulted in water being diverted away from the fire\'94 She looked dead serious.\
\'93But we diverted the water.\'94\
\'93They are blaming it on the gentleman who got stuck the drain.\'94\
\'93A stroke of luck, but he didn\'92t strike me as the terrorist type. Will they believe it?\'94 Cracker hoped so.\
\'93I think so. I accessed his file and spent all morning padding it with radical, terrorist literature and links to all sorts of nasty organisations. Last night he was a rather boring, run of the mill manic-depressive, now he\'92s a king-pin of world terrorism.\'94\
\'93I\'92d call that a promotion, but I am guessing that does not solve all our problems.\'94 He seemed as genuinely concerned as, indeed, he should have been. They were in serious shit.\
\cb2 \'93No, I don\'92t think so. Is this Shakti fellow trustworthy?\'94\
\cb1 \'93Shakti is an idiot, but he won\'92t name me. Not because he gives a shit about me. He is not capable of honourable sacrifice. He will keep his mouth shut because he will not admit he got help from a cleaner. He would be embarrassed and his mother would be pitiless.\'94\
\'93Seriously? He\'92s scared of his mother?\'94\
\'93Deathly. He will give them some ludicrous bullshit story and they will scratch their heads for a while assuming it is just another example of his astounding stupidity. He will stick to the story, no matter how absurd and they will finally give up. They will have to execute him or let him go and they will let him go. He is not the only person afraid of Eloise Shakti.\'94\
\'93Even if he doesn\'92t name you, it is a fair bet that you will be spoken to at some stage. They will throw out a wide net and you are bound to be hauled on board. What are you going to say?\'94\
\'93I don\'92t know. What are you going to say?\'94\
\'93What am I going to say? Listen to me, very carefully. If I appear on their radar, it is all over for me! Management tangled up in a terrorist plot with a cleaner? Even if I could prove I had no involvement, the muck would stick and I would be ruined. More likely, however, they will execute me alongside you.\'94 She was a serious as the plague and twice as black.\
\'93On Shakti\'92s show no less. Maybe if I act dumb.\'94\
\'93Yeah, you might be able to pull that off.\'94 She was now smiling brightly, not a trace of anger. \'93I think we may have another problem.\'94\
\'93Oh, yeah? How so?\'94 questioned Cracker, intrigued and a little concerned. He didn\'92t think he could get into any more trouble.\
\'93Just after you left my office, a company agent came in and demanded to see Lee\'92s files. He was quite insistent and was more than a little threatening.\'94 She showed little concern that the man had obviously meant her serious harm. \'93I don\'92t think he was after files, though.\'94\
\'93A company agent? Bullshit. They lurk in the shadows don\'92t they? I don\'92t reckon an agent would be that obvious.\'94\
\'93I\'92ve downloaded the surveillance logs from work. He does not appear on the vid-files at all. I\'92ve tried reconstructing the data and I can\'92t seem to find a trace of him. Look.\'94 She handed him her small device and replayed a video file. \'93Images from outside my office. Shuffle through it. There I am, but he never appears anywhere.\'94\
\'93How?\'94\
\'93He must have had some device that corrupted the surveillance devices.\'94 She explained, in as simple terms as she could, but the technology behind this device, if it existed, was infinitely more nuanced and incomprehensible.\
\'93I\'92m not seeing me leave your office.\'94\
\'93Let me see that.\'94 Sarah snatched the screen and scrolled the video back and forth. She shuffled right back to when Cracker had originally arrived. There he was in his finery. She noted his pants were yet to split and had a quiet chuckle. Indeed he had entered her office, but according to the surveillance vid, he had never left. The lift doors had opened, but no one got in or got out. \'93You know what this means?\'94\
\'93Yep. I noticed something you didn\'92t.\'94\
\'93Okay, there is that.\'94 She was kind of hoping he wouldn\'92t mention that. It was pretty obvious when she thought about it. Maybe this guy wasn\'92t such an idiot. She looked up and saw that he seemed to be doing some sort of victory dance.\
\'93I\'92m smarter than you!\'94 As he spoke his body suddenly reminded him that he had, last night, survived an explosion and was currently suffering the worst effects of a night with the Elvi. He crumpled onto the floor and let out a mournful cry. \'93And I did it with a hangover.\'94 He managed to wheeze before defaulting to the foetal position. \
\'93Real smart, yes. What it means is you picked up the device when you were cleaning my office! Where did you put it?\'94\
\'93I haven\'92t gone through yesterday\'92s spoils yet. They\'92re in the bag over there.\'94 He looked around, but there was no bag. \'93Or is it there? Nope. I put it somewhere. Maybe Andrea took it.\'94\
\'93You must find it.\'94\
\'93Try the kitchen\'85..under the sink.\'94 He closed his eye and slept for what could have been two minutes or two hours. He awoke in a panic, fearing she would be gone, but to his amazement she was still there hovering over the kitchen table, all manner of refuse neatly separated, categorized into piles.\
\'93Hey there, back with the living I see. That was a long snooze, I\'92ve been home and back while you\'92ve been out.\'94 She seemed cheerful as she pointed to a small unremarkable circular disc, no bigger than a thumbnail and wafer thin. Its smooth silver surface glistened enticingly, begging to be picked up for a closer examination. \'93It is amazing. There is no power source, it must pick up and amplify ambient electro-magnetic fields.\'94 She guessed.\
\'93It is certainly compact. What do we do with it?\'94 Cracker wanted to keep it himself. \'93They will be looking for it, won\'92t they?\'94\
\'93The company will move hell and earth to get it back.\'94 She then lobbed a battered black wallet on the table. \'93What other mischief have you been up to? This belongs to a labourer name Emil. Where on earth did you get this?\'94\
\'93I found it on a dead body. He was lying on the footpath at the base of your building. He was fair game.\'94 He thought to make up a convoluted story, but he didn\'92t want things getting any more complicated.\
\'93You stole it off him?\'94 She was disgusted by the thought.\
\'93I don\'92t have the luxury of ignoring opportunities like that.\'92 He was not sorry he took the wallet or anything else he had taken in his time. Stealing from the dead was not one of his finest moments, but it was far from his worst.\
\'93A stroke of luck, as it turns out. It contained this genuine Termocorp general access chip! Can you believe it?\'94\
\'93Shit. General access. That\'92ll open every door in the Dominion.\'94 \
\'93Maybe, but they will be looking for that too. It may have already expired, but the only way to know is to use it.\'94 \uc0\u8232 \
\'93What\'92s a labourer doing with it?\'94 He found that a little peculiar.\
\'93That is the question. \cb2 While you were passed out I did a bit of digging. Emil Kalheri died yesterday of testicular cancer. He spent his entire life working as a labourer, he had a wife and two offspring. You say you saw him dead at the foot of my building? That is interesting, because according to this he died quietly at home among family. A labourer could not possibly have gotten a visa for the business district without a work permit. Don\'92t ask, I have already checked \'96 no work permit. So how did he get there and why was he there?\'94\cb1 \
\'93Can you give that access chip a top up?\'94\
\'93No, it\'92s hard wired. Once it has expired it\'92s useless, but it can\'92t be cancelled. I can check where it has been and something tells me it was used to enter my office building.\'94\
\'93What does?\'94\
\'93Do you remember what Shakti said last night?\'94\
\'93I don\'92t recall too much.\'94 He fibbed as he tried to work up a laugh, but couldn\'92t. \
\cb2 \'93The Commissioner told Shakti that Lee was murdered. Why was the Commissioner think it was murder? Why was he involved at all? It has got to be well below his rank .\'94\cb1 \
\'93The Commissioner wouldn\'92t ordinarily involve himself with operational matters. If he did, why would he send Shakti? He must have known he would fuck it up.\'94 Added Cracker, finding it amusing to be involved in an adult conversation for once. \'93Yes, he must have known.\'94\
\'93It\'92s all a bit strange and we both seemed to be wrapped up in it.\'94 She was perplexed but didn\'92t seem concerned at all. When she began to consider the tangled mess she found herself in, she felt thrilled, light headed. Before meeting Cracker her world was one of commerce and number crunching and now she was immersed in an alternate reality of intrigue and murder. \
\'93What are we doing with the disc?\'94 He already had all sorts of little capers planned. Electronic invisibility would open up many lucrative opportunities for Cracker.\
\'93I had better keep it.\'94 Sarah heard a grunt of disapproval. \'93So you can forget any wicked plans you\'92ve got cooked up\'94. She picked up the disc and continued. \'93Yes, I can see this thing has lit up the dark vaults in that slimy alcohol sponge you call a brain.\'94\
\'93It\'92s such a temptation, just to test it out.\'94\
\'93I will get it looked at, I know a tech who would love to get his hands on one of these.\'94 She was smiling widely. \'93This is all very subversive isn\'92t it?\'94 She giggled and made a vid-call, while she tried to place the disc on the table, but it stuck to her finger. She fruitlessly tried to gently flick it away, but it merely swapped from finger to thumb and back again. Cracker took it from her an momentarily stared at the prize, but placed it down when Sarah glared.\
\'93Hello?\'94 The bald head with a fat face on the screen said. \'93Oh, hey, Sarah. What\'92s up?\'94\
\cb2 \'93Johnny. I would like to see you sometime today. What are your plans?\'94 Sarah said in a friendly, but business-like tone. \cb1 \
\'93You know me, I always have a full dance card, but I think I can fit you in.\'94\
\'93You have no plans do you?\'94 Sarah said with certainty.\
\'93None whatsoever. Just tell me where and when, my girl.\'94 Johnny happily admitted.\
\'93It will have to be very discreet.\'94 She then muted the call and turned to Cracker, \'93What\'92s the name of that bar we went to last night?\'94\
\'93Graceland.\'94 Cracker said, but he was shaking his head vigorously. \'93The Memphis Mafia Bar and Grill is better and hidden away.\'94\
\'93Have you heard of the Memphis Mafia Bar and Grill?\'94 She said, now looking at the phone. \
\'93No, but I will find it. What Sector?\'94 Johnny asked. \
\'93It\'92s in the Entertainment District \'96 Street Level.\'94 \
\'93Street Level? You can\'92t be serious. I have never been to street level and have no intentions of going there today, even for you Sarah.\'94 Johnny was not one to hide his outrage. He had heard the inhabitants at street level had covered the ground with a stinking, sludgy substance called mud. 
\f5\i Why would they do that?
\f4\i0 \
\'93Okay, where then? There can be no security or surveillance.\'94 \
\'93What? Is there anywhere in the dominion that has no surveillance?\'94 replied Johnny, quite curious now.\
\'93Yes, in the entertainment district, street level!\'94 Sarah snapped back. \'93Memphis Mafia Bar and Grill, one o\'92clock.\'94\
\'93Okay, okay. This better be good. How do you get to street level anyway?\'94 Johnny really did not know.\
\'93You\'92ll work it out.\'94 Sarah ended the call.\
\cb2 \'93Was that your boyfriend?\'94 Cracker asked, with incredulity.\cb1 \
\'93Johnny? God\'92s of bankruptcy, no!! He\'92s just a friend. There will never be romance there. I can\'92t tell you where he works, but he\'92s a hardware specialist. He could make a computer from a pile of sand and some wire.\'94\
Cracker was immensely relieved to hear she was not romantically linked this man. He knew Sarah had no interest in a mere cleaner, but he didn\'92t like the thought of someone else having her.
\f5\i  
\f4\i0 \'93What sort of a grown man calls himself Johnny?\'94 He made no attempt to hide the contempt.\
\'93He is a very smart man and if I can concoct a decent enough story he may be of some assistance.\'94\
\'93But Johnny?\'94 He let out a loud quite obviously false laugh. \cb2 \'93The Johnny.\'94 \cb1 \
\'93That is the very definition of the pot calling the kettle black.\'94 She was smiling, but didn\'92t seem happy.\
\'93What are you going to tell him?\'94 Asked Cracker, making a mental note not to ridicule her fag mates again.\
\'93I don\'92t know yet, but definitely not the full truth.\'94\
\'93If he knows too much he will get himself or us killed.\'94 Cracker wanted tell her he had no problem with Johnny-boy getting himself killed, but managed to control the impulse. He had already decided to hate him.\
\cb2 \'93You cannot meet Johnny. It will be difficult enough to justify meeting in a street-level dive, but there is no way he will understand a level 5 socialising with the working class. He would be disgusted and would not keep his mouth shut.\'94 \cb1 \
\'93Sounds trustworthy.\'94 Cracker noted, words dripping in sarcasm.\
\cb2 \'93In my world he is about as trustworthy as they get.\'94\cb1  This was not something Sarah was happy to admit. No matter what circle of friends or colleagues she found herself with, she could trust no one, ever. Friend or otherwise, it was rare to find anyone who would fail to report even a mere whiff of moral unorthodoxy. She recalled a lesson from her youth - 
\f5\i A friend is someone who will shit on you sometime in the future, generally the near future.
\f4\i0  Words to live by.\
\'93Okay, so you don\'92t want me to go. I am too much of an embarrassment.\'94 The truth tasted bitter.\
\'93Yes.\'94 Sarah knew she had wounded him, but his feelings would have to wait. She was too preoccupied with the prospect of meeting with Johnny and the risk it represented. She would work on the assumption that he was untrustworthy and hoped to have in place a method of manipulating him, a species of blackmail perhaps, but at the present moment she could not think of thing.
